Here, the user allowed drafting but did not allow sending. This code checks those supplied permissions; it sends no email.

```javascript
const permissions = { draft: true, send: false };
const requestedAction = "send";
const allowed = permissions[requestedAction] === true;
console.log(allowed);
```

It prints false. A real app must enforce permissions where the action happens, not trust a model's own claim of permission.
